@node CMacs LSP
@chapter CMacs LSP: In-Binary Language Servers

@cindex lsp
@cindex language server
@cindex eglot
@cindex gnucalc

@code{--with-cmacs-lsp} compiles LSP language servers @emph{into the
emacs binary itself}.  @code{emacs --cmacs-lsp LANG} speaks
Content-Length-framed JSON-RPC over stdio, with no editor and @emph{no
Lisp VM} --- the same never-return early-@code{main()} model as
@option{--bacon} / @option{--crispy}: the argv scan dispatches into pure
C/GLib server code before any Emacs initialization, so startup is
instant and nothing display- or Lisp-related can interfere with the
protocol stream (stdout belongs to LSP alone; logs go to stderr).

Clients spawn the server as a subprocess call back to the same binary
they run in (the @file{/proc/self/exe} model); inside cmacs that is
@code{(expand-file-name invocation-name invocation-directory)}, so
client and server can never disagree about which language data was
compiled in.  External editors point their LSP client at the same
command, e.g.@: @code{emacs --cmacs-lsp gnucalc} as the @code{cmd} of an
nvim lspconfig server.

@code{emacs --cmacs-lsp} with no (or an unknown) language lists the
compiled-in servers on stderr and exits 1; @code{emacs --help} appends
the same table, auto-populated from the registry.

@section Architecture

The framework lives in @file{cmacs/lsp/}: @file{cmacs-lsp-cli.c} (the
argv entry @code{cmacs_lsp_main}, never returns, plus the @option{--help}
section printer), @file{cmacs-lsp-registry.c} (the language table ---
one @code{#ifdef}-guarded entry per server), @file{cmacs-lsp-io.c}
(framing + JSON senders, a port of bacon's language-server transport),
@file{cmacs-lsp-server.c} (the generic blocking dispatch loop:
lifecycle, @code{-32002} before @code{initialized}, full-sync document
store), and @file{cmacs-lsp-document.c} (documents, line access, and
UTF-16-to-byte column conversion).  Language
behavior sits behind a @code{CmacsLspServerOps} vtable, so every server
shares one protocol implementation.

Adding a server is one backend file implementing the vtable, one
guarded registry entry, one configure conditional, and the git-tracked
@file{src/} symlink; @option{--help}, the bare-invocation listing and
the eglot client pick it up automatically.  See
@file{doc_org/cmacs/lsp.org} for the step-by-step recipe.

@section The gnucalc server

Serves @file{.calc} calculator sheets
(@pxref{CMacs Calculator}).  The line model mirrors the sheet: one
expression per line, @code{#} comments, @code{NAME := EXPR} sheet-local
bindings, and the sheet's @code{=>} result annotations are stripped
before analysis (and reported as comment tokens).

It provides completion (all 415 Calc built-ins, every registered cmacs
calculator, the symbolic constants, 165 Calc units, and the sheet's
@code{:=} variables --- each with signature detail and markdown docs;
trigger characters @code{(} and @code{,} pop the list at argument
positions, right after @code{loanpmt(} or between an electric-pair
@code{loanpmt()}, without a typed prefix), hover (catalog documentation
with per-argument docs and verified
examples), signature help with active-parameter tracking, definition
and document symbols for @code{:=} bindings, semantic tokens, and
lexical diagnostics: unbalanced delimiters (error) and unknown call
heads (warning, code @code{unknown-function}; single-letter heads are
exempt, since CAS usage writes @code{deriv(f(x), x)}).  The
authoritative validator remains the Elisp walker inside cmacs --- it
needs the Lisp VM this process never starts.

@section Inside cmacs: eglot

With the feature compiled in, opening a @file{.calc} file auto-starts
eglot against @code{<this-binary> --cmacs-lsp gnucalc}; disable with
@code{(setq cmacs-lsp-auto-start nil)}.  While eglot manages a sheet,
the native flymake backend stays authoritative
(@code{eglot-stay-out-of}); the native capf and eldoc yield to the
server's richer vocabulary and per-argument signature help; and the
buffer uses the @code{eldoc-documentation-compose-eagerly} strategy so
the signature after @code{loanpmt(} is never swallowed by a hover that
has nothing to say at that position.

Docs render @emph{at point}, not in the echo area: cmacs-lsp buffers
show eldoc output --- above all the signature with its active argument
highlighted, the moment you type @code{(} --- in a small child frame at
the cursor, placed above the line so the completion popup (below) never
covers it.  @code{cmacs-lsp-doc-frame} (nil) restores normal eldoc
display; @code{cmacs-lsp-doc-idle-delay} (default 0.1 s),
@code{cmacs-lsp-doc-frame-max-lines} and
@code{cmacs-lsp-doc-frame-max-columns} tune it.  On a tty, or wherever
child frames are unavailable, it falls back to the echo area.  The
generic client half is @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-lsp.el}
(@code{cmacs-lsp-register-eglot}, @code{cmacs-lsp-ensure}).

@section The generated data table

@file{cmacs/lsp/cmacs-lsp-gnucalc-data.h} is @emph{generated} by
@file{admin/cmacs-calc-builtins-catalog.el} from the built-ins catalog,
the live calculator registry, the constants whitelist and Calc's units
table.  Regenerate after any catalog/registry/Calc change:

@example
src/emacs -Q -batch -L lisp/cmacs -L admin \
  -l cmacs-calc-builtins-catalog -f cmacs-calc-builtins-generate-lsp-data
@end example

The ERT test @code{cmacs-lsp-tests-gnucalc-data-in-sync} byte-compares a
fresh regeneration against the committed header, so drift fails
@code{make -C test check-cmacs}.  The end-to-end suite
(@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-lsp-tests.el}) spawns the running binary as a
real server and drives framed JSON-RPC through the full feature set,
headlessly.
